}

lower: "Attention shape strategy: per-extent groups today, and planned flash attention" {
  style.fill: "#EEF1F8"
  style.stroke: "#CFD2DD"
  style.font-color: "#212121"
  style.font-size: 20
  style.bold: true
  style.border-radius: 10
  label.near: top-left
  grid-rows: 2
  grid-columns: 1
  grid-gap: 26

  strategies: " " {
    style.fill: "#EEF1F8"
    style.stroke: "#EEF1F8"
    grid-rows: 1
    grid-columns: 2
    grid-gap: 26

    # A markdown-labelled shape gets neither a border nor a width from D2, so the
    # border and the width live on a wrapping container and the prose sits inside
    # it as a blended child.
    dense: "A · Per-extent groups, the strategy shown above" {
      style.fill: "#E3E9FD"
      style.stroke: "#0d47a1"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
      style.font-size: 15
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 8
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 1
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 0
      width: 700

      dense_body: |md
        Gather each group's rows out of the flat list into a  
        dense batch, so every shape is fixed at compile time.  
        Needed because an offset computed from data does not  
        lower under compilation (torch-spyre#3508) — exactly  
        the arithmetic that flash relies on.

        · group the step's requests by padded length (extent)  
        · one compiled graph per plan does gather, SDPA and  
        store together  
        · the mask is [group, 1, 1, extent]: head and query axes  
        broadcast, since an encoder mask depends only on the  
        KV column

        **Shape axes: T for the body, (buffer_rows, group, extent)  
        for attention.**

        Costs: the pack is still paid, and fusion put buffer_rows  
        into the attention key, so warmup covers more variants.  
        Cheaper than a block-diagonal mask over the flat list,  
        which would compute every cross-request block and then  
        throw it away.
      | {
        style.fill: "#E3E9FD"
        style.stroke: "#E3E9FD"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        width: 620
      }
    }

    flash: "B · Flash-style attention, planned" {
      style.fill: "#E8F5E9"
      style.stroke: "#2e7d32"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
      style.font-size: 15
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 8
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 1
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 0
      width: 700

      flash_body: |md
        Never materialise the batch.  
        Each block of queries reads its request's offset from  
        the metadata at runtime and works on the flat list,  
        carrying a running softmax maximum and sum — the  
        online-softmax structure the decoder already uses over  
        KV pages.

        · no [group, 1, 1, extent] mask; boundaries are offsets  
        · no gather or store; the kernel reads the flat list  
        · extent disappears, replaced by a block count

        **Two separate prerequisites, often conflated:**  
        · torch-spyre#3508, data-dependent offsets → flat-list  
        attention, extent becomes a coarse block count  
        · symbolic shapes (the backend rejects SymInt today) →  
        no bucketing on any axis

        With #3508 alone the block count is still bucketed, as  
        the decoder's page loop is. buffer_rows also survives:  
        attention still writes into the body-sized output buffer.
      | {
        style.fill: "#E8F5E9"
        style.stroke: "#E8F5E9"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        width: 620
      }
    }
  }

  footer: " " {
    style.fill: "#EEF1F8"
    style.stroke: "#EEF1F8"
    grid-rows: 1
    grid-columns: 2
    grid-gap: 26

    simplifies: "If flash attention lands, what changes and what fusion already changed" {
      style.fill: "#FFF8E1"
      style.stroke: "#F9A825"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      style.font-color: "#5d4037"
      style.font-size: 15
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 8
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 1
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 0
      width: 1000

      simplifies_body: |md
        · attention stops gathering, so the pack and its launch go away  
        · extent collapses to a block count, coarser and cheaper to warm  
        · the mask disappears entirely; boundaries become offsets

        **What fusion already did, and what it cost:** collapsing gather,  
        SDPA and store into one graph removed two launches per plan per  
        layer, but pulled buffer_rows into the attention key. So the branch  
        moved toward flash's shape while moving away from its single axis.  
        Flash would not remove buffer_rows either — that needs attention to  
        stop writing into the body-sized buffer, or the body to stop being  
        bucketed.

        **Unchanged either way:** the padding seam, metadata that always  
        describes the real batch, warmup covering every reachable shape,  
        and the compilation mode.
      | {
        style.fill: "#FFF8E1"
        style.stroke: "#FFF8E1"
        style.font-color: "#5d4037"
        style.font-size: 14
        width: 920
      }
    }

    legend: "Legend" {
      style.fill: "#FFFFFF"
      style.stroke: "#CFD2DD"
      style.font-color: "#212121"
      style.font-size: 20
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 10
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 5
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 6

      L1: "Upstream vLLM, used as-is" {
        style.fill: "#37474f"
        style.stroke: "#263238"
        style.font-color: "#ffffff"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L2: "spyre-inference component" {
        style.fill: "#1565c0"
        style.stroke: "#0d47a1"
        style.font-color: "#ffffff"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L3: "Internal step · current path" {
        style.fill: "#E3E9FD"
        style.stroke: "#0d47a1"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L4: "Planned flash-attention path" {
        style.fill: "#E8F5E9"
        style.stroke: "#2e7d32"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L5: "Note or upstream dependency" {
        style.fill: "#FFF8E1"
        style.stroke: "#F9A825"
        style.font-color: "#5d4037"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

    }
  }
}
